From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Sun, 06 Sep 2026 01:08:01 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xen/arm: move the guest virtio-pci prefetch window somewhere a
 big BAR fits

The prefetchable virtio-PCI window is where a virtio-gpu host-visible blob lives,
which is how a guest gets Vulkan through Venus. Its old home -- 64 MiB at
0x3a000000, wedged between the magic pages and the start of RAM at 0x40000000 --
cannot hold a BAR bigger than 32 MiB, because a bigger BAR needs a bigger natural
alignment and there is nowhere below 0x40000000 to put one.

32 MiB is not enough. Measured on a Raspberry Pi 5 with a 32 MiB BAR: venus takes
~25 MiB of it for its command ring, its suballocation pools and the client's own
allocations, and the client's swapchain for a 1920x1080 surface then fails --

  MESA-VIRTIO: vn_AllocateMemory: VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY
  MESA-VIRTIO: vn_CreateSwapchainKHR: VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY

Move it to 0xc0000000, the 1 GB hole between the top of GUEST_RAM0 and the 4 GB
mark, and give it 768 MiB. That hole is the only one in this map big enough to hold
the window and leave it room to grow, and nothing else in this header falls inside
it.

768 MiB is three 256 MiB per-host slices, where the old 64 MiB gave eight 8 MiB slices
(VIRTIO_PCI_HOST_PREFETCH_MEM_SIZE in libxl_arm.c, which the companion patch grows
to 256 MiB). Trading slice count for slice size is deliberate: every guest in this
product is served by exactly one virtio-pci host, and eight 8 MiB windows are eight
windows no Vulkan client can use. The remaining 256 MiB of the hole is deliberately
left alone -- filling it would end the window exactly at 0x100000000, which is
where GUEST_VPCI_PREFETCH_MEM_ADDR (the passthrough-PCI prefetchable window)
begins, and an exclusive end that lands on another window's base is not something
this has been tested against.

The obvious-looking candidate, the 256 MiB at 0x10000000, is NOT free even though
its immediate neighbours suggest it is (the GICv3 redistributors end below
0x04100000 and GUEST_ACPI_BASE starts at 0x20000000): GUEST_VPCI_ECAM_BASE/SIZE is
0x10000000/0x10000000, exactly the same range. That is live code rather than a
reservation -- xen/arch/arm/vpci.c registers an ECAM MMIO handler over it for any
domain created with vPCI, and libxl_arm.c emits it into that guest's device tree.
Putting the blob window there is worse than an overlap, because the blob pages are
inserted into the guest p2m as real memory: an access to them never takes a stage-2
fault, so do_trap_stage2_abort_guest() -- the only path to try_handle_mmio() --
never runs and the ECAM handler never sees it. A guest with a passthrough PCI
device would read blob contents instead of PCI config space, and only for the pages
a blob happens to be mapped into, so intermittently. Hence 0xc0000000.

Why 0xc0000000 works:

 - it starts exactly where GUEST_RAM0 ends (0x40000000 + 2 GB), and a guest with
   more RAM than RAM0 holds gets the remainder at GUEST_RAM1 = 0x200000000, so no
   guest's RAM can ever reach it;
 - it is below the 4 GB mark, so a 32-bit BAR can still address it -- which is why
   the window was kept low in the first place;
 - it is 1 GB-aligned, so a BAR of any size up to the window can use it.

The window is already declared prefetchable (GUEST_VIRTIO_PCI_ADDR_TYPE_PREFETCH_MEM
is 0x42000000), and the guest device tree, libxl and the device model all derive
their view from these two values, so everything moves together. Guests without a
host-visible blob are unaffected: their virtio BARs are 16 KB each and fit anywhere.

The "64 MiB is reserved" comment above the definitions is updated with them, so the
header does not describe a size it no longer defines.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [the guest memory map is ABI; upstream would want
 this discussed, and probably a way for the toolstack to size the window]
---
 xen/include/public/arch-arm.h | 40 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 37 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/xen/include/public/arch-arm.h
+++ b/xen/include/public/arch-arm.h
@@ -501,10 +501,44 @@ typedef uint64_t xen_callback_t;
 #define GUEST_MAGIC_BASE  xen_mk_ullong(0x39000000)
 #define GUEST_MAGIC_SIZE  xen_mk_ullong(0x01000000)
 
-/* 64 MB is reserved for virtio-pci Prefetch memory */
+/* 768 MiB is reserved for virtio-pci Prefetch memory */
 #define GUEST_VIRTIO_PCI_ADDR_TYPE_PREFETCH_MEM    xen_mk_ullong(0x42000000)
-#define GUEST_VIRTIO_PCI_PREFETCH_MEM_ADDR         xen_mk_ullong(0x3a000000)
-#define GUEST_VIRTIO_PCI_PREFETCH_MEM_SIZE         xen_mk_ullong(0x04000000)
+/*
+ * The prefetchable window is where a virtio-gpu host-visible blob (Venus) lives,
+ * and its old home -- 64 MiB at 0x3a000000, wedged between the magic pages and the
+ * start of RAM -- cannot hold a BAR larger than 32 MiB: a bigger BAR needs a bigger
+ * natural alignment, and there is no room for one below 0x40000000. Measured on
+ * RPi5 with a 32 MiB BAR: venus takes ~25 MiB of it for its ring, pools and the
+ * client's own allocations, and the client's swapchain then fails with
+ * VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY for a 1920x1080 surface.
+ *
+ * 0xc0000000 is the 1 GB hole between the top of GUEST_RAM0 and the 4 GB mark: the
+ * window starts exactly where GUEST_RAM0 ends (0x40000000 + 0x80000000), and a
+ * guest with more RAM than RAM0 holds gets the remainder at GUEST_RAM1
+ * (0x200000000), so no guest's RAM can reach it. It is the only hole big enough to
+ * hold this window and let it grow, and nothing else in this header falls inside
+ * it. Being below 4 GB, a 32-bit BAR can still address it.
+ *
+ * Do NOT use 0x10000000: its neighbours make it look like a free 256 MiB hole (the
+ * GICv3 redistributors end below 0x04100000, GUEST_ACPI_BASE starts at
+ * 0x20000000), but GUEST_VPCI_ECAM_BASE/SIZE is exactly that range, and it is live
+ * -- vpci.c registers an ECAM MMIO handler over it and libxl emits it into the DT.
+ * Blob pages go into the guest p2m as real memory, so they never fault and the
+ * handler never sees the access: a guest with a passthrough PCI device would read
+ * blob contents instead of config space.
+ *
+ * 768 MiB is three 256 MiB per-host slices (VIRTIO_PCI_HOST_PREFETCH_MEM_SIZE in
+ * libxl_arm.c). The last 256 MiB of the hole is left alone so the window does not
+ * end exactly at 0x100000000, which is where GUEST_VPCI_PREFETCH_MEM_ADDR (the
+ * passthrough-PCI window) begins.
+ *
+ * The guest device tree, libxl and the device model all derive their view from
+ * these two values, so they move together. libxl_arm.c asserts that its slice size
+ * divides this size and leaves at least one slice, so a tree that has the libxl
+ * half of this change without this header fails to build rather than at run time.
+ */
+#define GUEST_VIRTIO_PCI_PREFETCH_MEM_ADDR         xen_mk_ullong(0xc0000000)
+#define GUEST_VIRTIO_PCI_PREFETCH_MEM_SIZE         xen_mk_ullong(0x30000000)
 
 #define GUEST_RAM_BANKS   2
 
